  

<h2 id="plain-text-capture-a-bauhaus-obsession-and-the-keyboard-that-finally-fits-my-edc">Plain text capture, a Bauhaus obsession, and the keyboard that finally fits my <span class="small-caps">EDC</span></h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/faa44b5c-f170-4ce3-ae26-8814bd170a29/pasted-percent20image-percent2020260518074217.png" alt="photos of a keyboard opened out, and folded; screenshots of an iOS clock widget in blue and pistachio green; screenshot of an app window with text (“Capture your notes, articles, links, quotations, journal entries and more… all without losing your spot.”)" width="2074" height="1216"> <em>Screenshots are from each company’s official website</em></p>
<hr>
<p>Today I’ll be sharing</p>
<ul>
<li>a new app for getting text into Obsidian</li>
<li>the iOS version of the Bauhaus Clock</li>
<li>the Nillkin Cube Pocket Foldable Bluetooth Keyboard with Trackpad</li>
</ul>
<p>No affiliates, just good stuff.</p>
<h3 id="capture---for-obsidian-macos">Capture - for Obsidian, macOS</h3>
<p>✅ One-off lifetime purchase</p>
<p>Capture is a brand new app from the entertaining and enigmatic Hiro.</p>
<p>Here’s what I noticed after playing with it for a bit:</p>
<ul>
<li>Capture is fast at getting things to where you want them in Obsidian, because you don’t have to choose from a list</li>
<li>Killer feature = context; Capture automatically takes note of where you were when making the note</li>
<li>I’d like more choice when appending, like adding the date as well as the time for those of us with an <span class="small-caps">OBTF</span></li>
<li>I’d like to also be able to prepend (✅ <span class="small-caps">ADDED</span>)</li>
<li>No tags are available (yet) when capturing to a specific note</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Conclusion</strong>: Capture is fast and fun, but <a href="https://ellanew.com/2025/02/10/ptpl-143-six-drafts-alternatives" class="wikilink">Drafts is still king</a>. You might like to check out Hiro’s semi-regular <a href="https://www.hiro.report" target="_blank">Hiro Report</a> for<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>Delightful tech, Apple apps, everyday gear, and other fun finds from the web straight to your inbox.”</p>
<h3 id="bauhaus-clock-now-has-an-ios-version">Bauhaus Clock now has an iOS version!</h3>
<p>✅ One-off lifetime purchase</p>
<p><em><span class="pull-double">“</span>Same obsessive craft, new canvas”</em></p>
<p>Just a beautiful product. I bought the Mac version of the Bauhaus Clock at the end of last year, and the iOS version almost as soon as it was released.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://bauhausclock.com" target="_blank">Mac Bauhaus Clock</a> is my screensaver, and the <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/bauhaus-clock-analog-watch/id6755106534" target="_blank">iOS version</a> has mostly replaced <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/desk-clock-analog-clock-face/id1253066126" target="_blank">Desk Clock</a> (still highly recommended) on my iPhone. I like being able to adjust the skin of the Bauhaus Clock widget independent of the one I’ve chosen for the app itself.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion</strong>: This is an unapologetically expensive app that you totally do not need, but may well want anyway. There is something highly satisfying about being surrounded by well-designed things that were made with fastidious care and attention to detail!</p>
<h3 id="nillkin-travel-keyboard-folding">Nillkin travel keyboard (folding)</h3>
<p>✅ Around $100 <span class="small-caps">AUD</span> (I got mine for just under $80)</p>
<p>Logitech’s <span class="small-caps">K380</span> Bluetooth keyboard can connect to three different devices at the press of a button, but it’s relatively heavy. Apple’s Bluetooth keyboard is lovely, but it doesn’t fit in my everyday carry bag (<span class="small-caps">EDC</span>).</p>
<p>At only 182 x 108 x 16 mm when folded, it’s easy to carry with you. It has 64 keys compared with Apple’s 78, but most of the expected keyboard features are available via the Function key.</p>
<p>The Nillkin Cube Pocket Foldable Bluetooth Keyboard with Touchpad</p>
<ul>
<li>can connect to three different devices</li>
<li>is small and light</li>
<li>fits into my <span class="small-caps">EDC</span></li>
<li>has a dual function numpad / trackpad</li>
</ul>
<p>I’m happy with it, though of course there are a few<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">’</span>but’s.</p>
<ul>
<li>iOS is a poor cousin; more features are available when using it with Android devices</li>
<li>The trackpad works but isn’t smooth when scrolling (iOS)</li>
<li>You need to place it on a stable surface when typing, due to the folds (on-lap writing sessions work, crossed-legs don’t)</li>
<li>It comes in a white drawstring cloth bag, which I expect will look dirty fairly soon (though it still looks good after knocking about in my handbag for a couple of weeks, surprisingly)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Conclusion</strong>: I like this keyboard for its supreme portability and the way the keys are protected when folded. It works well enough, but I wish the trackpad was more iOS-friendly.</p>
